/-
# Chains of divisors with `d_{i+1} ≡ 1 (mod d_i)` (Erdős Problem 696)

Erdős Problem 696: let `h(n)` be the largest `ℓ` such that there is a sequence of primes `p₁ < ⋯ <
p_ℓ` all dividing `n` with `p_{i+1} ≡ 1 (mod p_i)`. Let `H(n)` be the largest `u` such that there is
a sequence of integers `d₁ < ⋯ < d_u` all dividing `n` with `d_{i+1} ≡ 1 (mod d_i)`. Estimate `h(n)`
and `H(n)`. Is it true that `H(n) / h(n) → ∞` for almost all `n`?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import MathlibExt.Data.Set.Density

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.NumberTheory.DivisorChainLengthErdosProblem696Wanted

/-!
Source: T. F. Bloom, Erdős Problems, Problem #696, https://www.erdosproblems.com/696 (status SOLVED
(LEAN)). UnsolvedMath record UM-00002265 (`EP-696`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

"Almost all" means natural density `1` (`Set.HasDensity`, MathlibExt). Chains are lists with
`List.IsChain` for `a < b ∧ b ≡ 1 [MOD a]`; `h` and `H` are `sSup`s of lengths, bounded for `n ≥ 1`.
`log_* x` is the number of applications of the natural logarithm needed to reach a value `≤ 1`. Only
the estimate is formalized: the problem page records it but gives no answer to the question whether
`H(n) / h(n) → ∞` for almost all `n`, so that question is not stated. The estimate is proved by
machine-generated proofs (GPT 5.5); the problem page marks the problem solved. Compared with the
formal-conjectures statement `ErdosProblems/696.lean` (formal-conjectures states the question as
refuted, citing `H(n) / h(n) = 2 + o(1)` for almost all `n`, which the page text does not state; it
does not state the estimate; no code imported).
-/

/-- `h(n)`: the largest `ℓ` such that there are primes `p₁ < ⋯ < p_ℓ` dividing `n` with
`p_{i+1} ≡ 1 (mod p_i)` (a bounded set of lengths for `n ≥ 1`). -/
noncomputable def primeChainLength (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=
  sSup {ℓ | ∃ L : List ℕ, L.length = ℓ ∧ L.IsChain (fun a b => a < b ∧ b ≡ 1 [MOD a]) ∧
    ∀ p ∈ L, p.Prime ∧ p ∣ n}

/-- `H(n)`: the largest `u` such that there are integers `d₁ < ⋯ < d_u` dividing `n` with
`d_{i+1} ≡ 1 (mod d_i)` (a bounded set of lengths for `n ≥ 1`). -/
noncomputable def divisorChainLength (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=
  sSup {u | ∃ L : List ℕ, L.length = u ∧ L.IsChain (fun a b => a < b ∧ b ≡ 1 [MOD a]) ∧
    ∀ d ∈ L, d ∣ n}

/-- The iterated logarithm `log_* x`: the number of times the natural logarithm must be applied to
`x` to reach a value at most `1`. -/
noncomputable def logStar (x : ℝ) : ℕ :=
  sInf {k : ℕ | Real.log^[k] x ≤ 1}

/-- The estimate recorded on erdosproblems.com/696: for almost all `n`,
`log_* n ≪ h(n) ≤ H(n) ≪ log_* n`, i.e. there are constants `c > 0` and `C` such that the set of
`n` with `c log_* n ≤ h(n)` and `H(n) ≤ C log_* n` has natural density `1`. -/
def divisorChainLengthNormalOrderLogStar : Prop :=
  ∃ c C : ℝ, 0 < c ∧ {n : ℕ | c * logStar n ≤ primeChainLength n ∧
    (divisorChainLength n : ℝ) ≤ C * logStar n}.HasDensity 1

/--
Resolved: erdosproblems.com/696 records that, by proofs generated by GPT 5.5, `log_* n ≪ h(n) ≤ H(n)
≪ log_* n` for almost all `n`, and lists the problem as solved. Source: T. F. Bloom, Erdős Problems,
Problem #696, https://www.erdosproblems.com/696 (status SOLVED (LEAN)); resolution:
erdosproblems.com/696, proofs generated by GPT 5.5 (see the page's comments; thanks to W. van Doorn,
D. Turturean and Treasure42), erdosproblems.com/696: 'It is now known, via proofs generated by GPT
5.5, that for almost all n, log_* n ≪ h(n) ≤ H(n) ≪ log_* n'; status SOLVED (LEAN),
https://www.erdosproblems.com/696.
-/
public theorem_wanted divisorChainLengthNormalOrderLogStar_holds :
    divisorChainLengthNormalOrderLogStar

end MathlibExt.NumberTheory.DivisorChainLengthErdosProblem696Wanted
